# The Atlee Constituent Assembly

## First Scene

The Atlee Constituent Assembly has met in New Delhi! The country believes and calls it the Constituent Assembly. We, however, have inscribed it as the Atlee Constituent Assembly. This is neither a printing error nor an expression of party prejudice. We gave it this name only after reflecting inwardly on what the outcome of that Assembly would be.

The Constituent Assembly met in New Delhi at eleven in the morning on the ninth. Gandhi was not in it. Nor was Mr. Jinnah.

The man who presided over the Assembly was Dr. Sachchidananda Sinha. He was not in the Congress. Disliking its course and programme, he had left it in 1936.

He delivered the presidential address. Yet it was not in Hindi, which Congressmen fondly dream of as the national language, but in the English language of the white man!

Dr. Sinha gave an interesting address on what the new polity and self-government should be like, and what constitution should be taken as an example and guide in drafting a constitution for self-rule. It contained explanations and references to the American, Swiss, French, and several other constitutional systems. Every plan was discussed except the scheme of Rama Rajya recommended by Gandhi and accepted by Congressmen.

Speeches praised our country, India, but there was no mention of Bharat Varsha or Mother Bharat!

The presidential address quoted not the Gita or the Ramayana, but Iqbal’s poetry and the Gospel of Jesus.

The president worked only in the morning. Doctors had apparently ordered him not to work in the afternoon.

He was only the temporary chairman. Rajendra Prasad has been elected permanent president.

Thus the Constituent Assembly presented several “interesting” incidents. But a man from the United Provinces defeated all their flavours with his heroic flavour.

He seems to have been angered by the things recorded above. Like the average Congressman, he imagined that the Constituent Assembly would be a sacred undertaking in which, with Mother Bharat’s aid, every proceeding would be conducted in the national language Hindi to create Rama Rajya for Bharat Varsha. His name was R. V. Dhulekar.

He began his speech. “All the rules and plans of this Constituent Assembly must be written in Hindi, and the debate too must be in Hindi,” he said. He spoke in Hindi.

The chairman intervened and asked him to speak in English. “I cannot,” he replied in Hindi. “There are members here who do not know Hindi; they will not understand what you say,” said the chairman. He did not stop with that reason. “Look, Comrade Rajagopalachari is here. He does not know Hindi. How can he understand if you speak in Hindi?” he asked.

Dhulekar flew into a proper rage. “If they are unfortunate enough not to know Hindustani, they have no place not merely in the Constituent Assembly but in India itself!” he declared, and launched forth rapidly. He said it was wrong to cite the British and other foreign parliaments as examples; India’s constitutional plan must be discussed in an Indian language and in keeping with the Indian spirit. The chairman stopped him, saying his speech was out of order.

“I shall speak.”

“It is improper for you to speak.”

“I am a member of this Assembly. I have the right to speak here.”

“I am chairman of the Assembly. I therefore have authority to bring you to order.”

So ran the argument between the chairman and Dhulekar.

“Sit down, sir!” the chairman said rather sharply.

Diwan Chaman Lall and Pandit Nehru led the still-speaking Dhulekar back to his seat as though driving a goat. Do not be angry and think that we impudently supplied the word “goat”; that was the description in *The Hindu* of December 11.

These were not the only curiosities in the Assembly gathered to determine India’s future. Since the Muslim League did not participate, one section of seats stood empty. Every Constituent Assembly convened anywhere in the world until now has appeared either as the child of revolution or as a garden where new rights bloom. Here, however, it met while an existing division remained unresolved and while the League, which shared power in the government and held importance in its programme, withheld cooperation. Ceremony, spectacle, heroism, humour, and romance may therefore be seen in it. Will any result emerge? Only a difficult sigh can answer. Why? Because this Constituent Assembly is not a true Constituent Assembly.

What do we mean by saying “this Constituent Assembly”? We shall explain. Before doing so, let us state that the word “this” must not be taken as a sign of our own likes and dislikes. We are using Pandit Nehru’s own word.

Arrangements for convening the Constituent Assembly continued while the League maintained that it would not participate. What did the Congress do? It advertised its strength by declaring that the Assembly would meet whether others came or not. It showed only that it had dared to ignore the League, not that it possessed the strength and tact needed to reconcile the League and Congress.

Did Britain not understand that meeting while this split persisted would only deepen bitterness and division? Was this difficult to grasp? Imperialists, who have turned even gambling into a great art, understood it well and rejoiced. Had they possessed goodwill, they would have said that the Assembly should be postponed briefly, until the situation improved and affection had an opportunity to grow. But imperialism is a wedge driven into domestic divisions. It delighted in them. It said, “We shall not prevent the Constituent Assembly from meeting,” and handed Congress a cup of sugar-cane juice to drink. Noakhali on one side, Bihar on the other, and amid that condition an Assembly meets to prepare the future constitution!

Once the decision to convene it had been made and Britain had supplied the accompanying music, could Britain not have said, “Very well, let it meet,” and attended to other work? No. It set aside everything else and engaged in work suited to its nature. The Assembly was about to meet, and the League would not participate. The League’s absence must not be allowed to look commonplace; it had to be announced to the world. The existing bitterness between the two parties had to be increased a little. What finer opportunity could there be? With only a few days left before the Assembly met, Britain instructed Viceroy Wavell to bring representatives of the League, Congress, and Sikhs to London.

We condemn not merely the manner in which responsible leaders charged with deciding the polity of a great land were made to run at a summons. Could “consultation” held at the very last moment produce a result anywhere? If a result was genuinely desired, was this the method? No extraordinary intelligence is needed to detect the diplomacy hidden in this hasty invitation.

They invited them. Why? The question arose. For nothing, friends! The British Government said, “We invite you only to offer whatever assistance and advice we can so that the Assembly about to meet may proceed successfully.” What astonishing affection and concern, suddenly welling up!

Pandit Nehru asked: What question was there to discuss in London? Where was the time? Why the haste? Would the convening of the Constituent Assembly be delayed? He declared that he would not go to London.

At once every Congress newspaper in the country wrote that he must not go, that there was no need, that the League might carry its kavadi to London if it wished, but Congress would not go.

Before the ink of those exceedingly heroic declarations had dried, imperialism threw another move of the dice.

“Nehruji, I request you kindly to come. Your visit will not delay the meeting of the Assembly. We shall make arrangements so that you may leave in time for it. Come!” A telegram came from Atlee to Nehru.

At once the resolution changed. “Nehru is going to London; Atlee has requested him,” they began to sing.

Britain, long practised in enslaving many countries, knows the mentality of an enslaved nation. At the right moment, if a kind word mixed with a little affection is uttered in the form of praise, it tastes exceedingly sweet to a country worn down by bondage. Even if it is an empty deception, it is enough that it sounds sweet.

They say that a camel loaded with an enormous burden lies down, thinking it cannot carry so much. It means to resist its master. But the master is a trickster. He removes the last item loaded upon it. The camel is delighted: “We stubbornly refused to carry the burden; the master yielded and removed the load. Victory is ours!” It rises briskly and walks. Long years of alien rule have implanted this camel nature in us. Imperialism knows it well; it pats us, sometimes smiles, and gets its work done.

Atlee’s personal affectionate invitation was one such device. It gave Congress an opportunity to say joyfully and proudly, “Our Nehru goes only because Atlee himself pleaded with him.” It also gave the League and its supporters an opportunity to say, “See, the white man is pleading with Nehru, who said he could not come!” The rolling of hostile eyes between the two parties continued.

“It is not our intention to prevent the Constituent Assembly from meeting, nor to fail to implement or alter the plan given by the British ministers,” Atlee informed Nehru.

Pandit Nehru would naturally be pleased to hear the British Prime Minister say that the Assembly would meet and the ministers’ plan would be implemented. He agreed and journeyed overseas.

Mr. Jinnah observed Atlee lavishing this favour upon Nehru. Could he remain quiet? Did Atlee even allow him to? No. He sent Jinnah a copy of the message sent to Nehru. Jinnah immediately telegraphed London:

“At nine o’clock on the night of November 29, I was given a copy of the message you sent Pandit Nehru. But the correspondence between you and the Pandit was not supplied. This alters the situation that existed when I agreed to come to London. If only the matter mentioned in your letter to Pandit Nehru is to be discussed in London, there is no need for us to come. Unless an opportunity is given to discuss the entire situation, our journey to London will serve no purpose. Clarify the position immediately.”

Atlee replied to Jinnah’s telegram: “We are meeting to consider every aspect of the situation. You have misunderstood the telegram I sent Nehru. Come to London.”

This is not mere amusement; it is diplomacy.

When Nehru asked whether there was any important question to discuss, Atlee said he invited him only to discuss arrangements for the smooth functioning of the Assembly, not to prevent it or disrupt the plan. When Jinnah replied that he could not come merely for such a limited discussion, Atlee said, “I invite you to discuss the entire matter.” Each received the form he desired! They say God appears to devotees in whichever form they wish to behold Him. That is also the method of British imperialism.

Thus Atlee invited the two parties after planting two different thoughts and desires in their minds.

A reconciliation conference in Britain; in India, talk of stopping the sword with its scabbard! On the stage, the actor playing Satyavan may sing to Savitri, “My beloved, I shall never part from you; my heart will not bear it!” Yet backstage he may say, because her singing voice is poor, “Only when this nuisance is gone will the company prosper.” Reading in the same newspapers of the reconciliation effort in Britain and the heroic speeches delivered here at that very time recalls the theatre.

They went overseas for reconciliation. Observe how the setting was arranged. Jinnah and Liaquat stayed in one hotel; Nehru and Baldev Singh in another. The matter they had gone to discuss was “reconciliation”!

What happened? What else could happen? What India’s enemies desired took place. There was no settlement, only another round-table conference. Nehru returned and said the journey had been futile.

It succeeded in increasing bitterness, but did it accomplish anything? Not for either party. Imperialism won.

Pretending to issue a declaration arising from this reconciliation drama, the British revealed imperialism’s intention: since a particular community had not participated in the Constituent Assembly, they would not impose the constitution drafted there upon people or regions that refused to accept it.

What does that mean? Congress is free to convene and conduct the Constituent Assembly and draft and debate a constitution whether or not the League participates.

The League has the right to participate or remain outside. Equal weight, what affection!

Congress has the right to draft a constitution; the League has the right to say it will not touch it; and imperialism claims the “right” to say it will not impose a constitution disliked by a particular party. Who has won? Think calmly and at length, young people!

There are already numerous constitutional schemes that were drafted but never accepted and now remain as handsome volumes in libraries.

Mylai Iyengar prepared a constitution for self-rule; it became a book. Mrs. Besant prepared one; that too became a book. The Nehru Report prepared by Jawaharlal’s father met the same fate. The Constituent Assembly is useful only for producing one more book! Imperialism has made that plain.

A constitution may be prepared, but it must be made acceptable. British imperialism, which will not release its grip, will issue a White Paper saying regretfully: “Since one hundred million Muslims refuse to accept the constitution, we fear that imposing it would injure democracy, and therefore we cannot bring it into force.”

What purpose, then, has the work performed served? Why should I say it? The mere thought makes our heads bow involuntarily with shame and grief.

In a nation’s history, the meeting of a Constituent Assembly is an inspiring event, a time to rejoice that freedom has been won. It equals the joy of a woman suspected of being barren when the child born to her first calls “Mother!” A Constituent Assembly meeting as the sign of liberation from a century and a half of slavery should be such an event. In what condition does this rare occurrence, deserving inscription in history, stand today?

The newspapers report that there was joy in the Constituent Assembly and that those who came conversed warmly. Heroic writers declared, “What if the League tried to stop it? The Assembly has met!” But one part of the hall remains empty.

Again the theatre comes to mind. If one row of the auditorium is empty, the theatre manager will not mention it publicly, though inwardly every actor will be troubled. Outside he will strain to smile and say, “That part of the hall was empty, but we did not worry. Did the play stop because some people did not come? It went on!” Likewise, one cannot see a section of the Constituent Assembly empty without inward anguish, yet one cannot speak of it openly. A difficult position!

Imperialism rejoices: “The Constituent Assembly meets, but there is no danger. The League is absent, so we can brush its constitution aside.” Those who should tremble, fear, and grieve are happy. Imperialism, which ought to despair, is delighted. We, who should unite, act, and attain an incomparable victory, take pleasure in boasting, “Why should I hear what you say, and why should you hear me?” Time is wasted; deceit triumphs.

Congress friends may say, “If the British refuse to accept the constitution we prepare, we shall conduct a final struggle.” That would be a sign of courage, not an announcement of victory. If, even after the Assembly has met and dispersed, another struggle must be waged, why should the Assembly meet? Suppose we begin that struggle. Would the League and Congress not remain divided even then? Suppose, without concern for that, we fight, win, and drive out imperialism. Even after victory, would there not inevitably remain a struggle to end the internal conflict? Is it good for this condition to grow? Young people must think about this. That is why we grieve that the meeting of the Constituent Assembly, which should have been a mighty event, is instead helping to deepen bitterness.

It has already met; there is no use merely finding fault now. But an opportunity still remains.

Only ceremonial business, an inspiring speech, the election of the president, and such matters have occurred. The Assembly will meet again to work. Congress must use this interval to meet the League, negotiate a settlement, and make mutual concessions. If the Constituent Assembly is not to end as futile labour, and if it is to produce the desired benefit, no seats must remain empty when the stage of action begins.

To put fear into imperialism’s eyes, the gap visible in the Assembly today must disappear. We must shatter its hope that this Assembly will become a body that strengthens the Congress-League division. We must prove that we can not only fight imperialism but also reach an accommodation with our own brethren. Only then will convening the Constituent Assembly have meaning. Otherwise, there will be no difference between the Meerut Congress and the Delhi Constituent Assembly.

The League leader asks: Congress may prepare a constitution for Congress constituencies, but who will accept it if it prepares one for the League’s constituencies? He calls today’s Constituent Assembly a mere spectacle.

Congress sympathizers naturally become angry when a League leader speaks thus. But what use is anger? His point is that this Assembly will be of no practical use. Is that not true? The British have already declared that they will not impose the constitution upon those who do not want it. What use is there in drafting it after that? One of two things must be done:

1. A firm assurance must at least be obtained from the British Government that the constitution drafted will be brought into effect.

2. Or, before drafting it, the League’s consent must somehow be secured.

If neither is done, and if one rejoices merely that the Assembly has met while professing unconcern both for the League’s course and the present British declaration, joy alone will remain and the benefit will be zero. Refusing to recognize this is refusing to see the truth. Once it is recognized, it becomes clear that the League leader’s statement was not wrong.

For friends who still say they cannot see it, we draw attention to a profound but partly concealed meaning in a statement by Pandit Jawaharlal:

“It cannot be said that the Constituent Assembly now meeting is the final one. It may become necessary to convene another Constituent Assembly.”

What can that mean? Think about it without anger, calmly and at length, with the desire that problems be resolved, with the determination that divisions be ended, and with the resolve that we must defeat in diplomacy too the imperialism that mocks our divisions. Why did Pandit Nehru say that this Constituent Assembly, convened with such difficulty, would not suffice and that another might have to be convened?

“I have no particular fascination for this Constituent Assembly.

“I do not in any sense regard this as the final Constituent Assembly.

“After India obtains a greater measure of freedom, it may become necessary to convene another Constituent Assembly.”

These words were spoken by Pandit Jawaharlal at the Meerut Congress and appeared in *The Hindu* of November 22. What do they mean?

Why should Nehru, who spent his whole life in India’s struggle for freedom, say he had no “fascination” for the Constituent Assembly described as the instrument for drafting freedom’s constitution? Why speak as though it was not the object he sought, that the true object was still unavailable, and that something else had been obtained but would do for now? Friends, what did the Pandit mean by “this Constituent Assembly”? What thought lies within the word “this”? He also said, “I do not regard this as the final Assembly.” That implies there is another, final Assembly. What is it? Why is this not that Assembly?

Whether the League comes or not, this remains only *this* Constituent Assembly. That is why Nehru, who desires freedom, said he had developed no extraordinary attachment to it.

Only an Assembly convened in circumstances where it can be said that the League too participated will be final. That is why Nehru says he does not regard this one as final.

Pandit Nehru understands that an Assembly unable to attain finality because the League has not joined cannot yield India’s complete freedom. That is why he says that, after India obtains greater freedom, another Constituent Assembly will meet.

We now ask you to reflect on the condition of this Constituent Assembly. A League leader called it a mere drama, and that angers you. Very well, put his words aside. Why does Pandit Nehru himself consider this Constituent Assembly insufficient? Because, being well acquainted with the histories of many countries, he knows that a constitutional plan rejected by one hundred million Muslims, living together in large numbers across many provinces of the Indian subcontinent and organized under the disciplined banner of one political party, cannot be enforced without permanent military rule, a dictatorship stronger than any the world has seen, or the cooperation of British imperialism. He speaks with that knowledge. Along with patriotism, he has studied the histories of many nations!

15-12-1946

---NOTES---
The Tamil title is a deliberate satirical renaming of the “Constituent Assembly” as the “Atlee Constituent Assembly”; it is not treated as an OCR error. The printed subtitle “First Scene” is retained, although no separate second-scene OCR work was found under a related filename in the canonical source folder. Names and historical references were normalized only in English: Sachchidananda Sinha, R. V. Dhulekar, Diwan Chaman Lall, Wavell, Liaquat, Baldev Singh, Noakhali, Bihar, and the Meerut Congress. One badly OCR-damaged English quotation attributed to *The Hindu* on page 3 could not be recovered reliably from the Tamil OCR and is represented by the surrounding Tamil account rather than invented as a verbatim quotation. The Tamil OCR source remains unchanged in the bilingual output.
